# R16: sensitivity analyses and item-level acceptance moved to appendices
POINTER = "Sensitivity analyses with all 10 pairs, with each pair left out in turn, with the verb–video correspondence values as a covariate, and with frequentist models led to the same conclusions (Appendix B). Acceptance of each RVC and V1 description is reported in Appendix C. In zero-result events, the stronger-association V1 was accepted less often than its weaker-association partner in seven of the eight pairs by children and in six by adults, so the association effect was carried by most pairs rather than by one or two."
APPC_PAR = 'Table C1 shows acceptance of each RVC and V1 description by result realization for children, pooled across the two age groups, and adults. In zero-result events, in seven of the eight pairs, the stronger-association V1 was accepted less often than its weaker-association partner by children, and in six of the eight pairs by adults; the remaining adult pairs were at ceiling for both V1s (Pair 2) or for both groups (Pair 9). The association effect was thus carried by most pairs rather than by one or two. Pair 9 (*cā* "wipe" vs. *chōng* "rinse" a mirror) behaved differently from the other pairs: Both V1s were accepted in all zero-result trials, and children also accepted the RVC descriptions of the zero-result videos in 50% (*cā-gānjìng*) and 75% (*chōng-gānjìng*) of trials, as did one of the five adults who judged *cā-gānjìng*. In the videos of this pair, the stain on the mirror was a small red spot in one corner and was not very conspicuous, so that whether the mirror had become clean was difficult to see; one child explained a rejection by saying that only one side of the mirror had been wiped. Acceptance of the RVC descriptions in zero-result events was otherwise low for every pair (children: 0%–38%; adults: 0%).'
